    "description": "The land surface representation in LM4.5 builds on the representation in LM4.1 (Shevliakova et al. 2024) with a number of significant revisions. LM4.1 captures biophysical and biogeochemical processes throughout multi-layer vegetation canopy, snow, and soil columns. The number of canopy layers is prognostic as determined in the Perfect Plasticity Approximation (PPA) dynamic vegetation model. The snow model has fixed six layers which could have zero snow. The soil model has 20 layers representing 10 m of soil, including a prognostic water table.  The model also includes representation of lakes, ice sheets with prescribed glacier mask, river transport via routing scheme. In LM4.5 all land components (e.g. vegetation, soil, snow, rivers, lakes) are fully coupled and exchange energy and moisture with the atmosphere and ocean (via runoff).  Vegetation, litter, and soils exchange carbon with the atmosphere and ocean. LM4.5 represents sub-grid scale heterogeneity of the land surface using a mosaic approach that splits each land grid cell, from the top of canopies throughout the soil column cell into multiple tiles. Each tile represents a fraction of the grid cell with unique physical, hydrological, ecological, and biogeochemical properties. The number of tiles in a grid cell is dynamic, driven by human disturbances (changes in land use from CMIP7 historical reconstructions and future scenarios), fire (assumed to be wildfire on primary and abandoned agricultural and logged lands), and gap dynamics resulting from forest canopy tree mortality.  The model incorporates a new soil carbon scheme, Global Integrated Microbial Interactions with Carbon in Soil (GIMICS; Lee et al., submitted), which explicitly represents microbial dynamics and physicochemical stabilization mechanisms for both surface litter and coarse woody debris and vertically discretized soils that distinguish the rhizosphere from bulk soil. GIMICS also accounts for spatially and vertically varying soil temperature and moisture effects on microbial activity and organic matter turnover, and represents C transport via bioturbation, diffusion, advection, and lateral export to rivers. Consequently, the terrestrial carbon budget now includes an explicit representation of Dissolved Organic Carbon (DOC) export by rivers to the ocean.  The model includes a new snow scheme (GLASS, Zorzetto et al. 2024, Zorzetto et al. 2025) that includes representation of snow aging, compaction, and evolution of microphysical properties (grain shape and size), as well as effects of the interactively calculated deposition of light-absorbing particles and evolution of their concentration in the snowpack on snow reflectance.  The representation of fires has been enhanced by implementation of prognostic lightning ignition from the atmospheric convection scheme rather than an external prescribed file. Also, the fire scheme provides the fluxes of fire-related tracers to the atmospheric component. The fire scheme prognostically calculates fire radiative power that serves as an input to determine the vertical distribution of fire injection heights (Pouyaei et al., in review).  The representation of agriculture has been enhanced to treat rain-fed and irrigated croplands as distinct categories, with a separate sub-grid tile representing each category if they co-exist in the grid cell. Also, the planting and harvesting dates are calculated dynamically using accumulated climatological conditions for rain-fed and irrigated crops, with double-cropping where the conditions are suitable (Phillipps et al., in preparation).  The parameterization of the surface fluxes uses interactive parameters of in-canopy turbulence profile, as well as changes to the other turbulence-related parameters (Ghannam et al., in preparation). Additionally, the treatment of surface fluxes now includes physically-based representation of the resistance of the laminar layer near the soil surface.  Shevliakova, E. S. Malyshev, I. Martinez-Cano, P. C. D. Milly, S. W. Pacala, P. Ginoux, K. A. Dunne, J. P. Dunne, C. Dupuis, K. L. Findell, K. Ghannam, L. W. Horowitz, T. R. Knutson, J. P. Krasting, V. Naik, P. Phillipps, N. Zadeh, Y. Yu, F. Zeng, and Y. Zeng (2024): The Land Component LM4.1 of the GFDL Earth System Model ESM4.1: Model Description and Characteristics of Land Surface Climate and Carbon Cycling in the Historical Simulation. Journal of Advances in Modeling Earth Systems, 16, No.5, doi:10.1029/2023ms003922.  Lee, M. E. Shevliakova, S. Malyshev (2026)  Implications of Microbial Dynamics and Soil Physicochemical Stabilization for Global Soil Carbon Sequestration Potential. Submitted to JAMES  Pouyaei, A. S. Malyshev, R. Wang, E. Shevliakova, and P. Ginoux (2026) Linking Prognostic Fire Emissions to Atmospheric Chemistry and Aerosols in GFDL Atmospheric and Land Model version 4.2 (AM4.2/LM4.2). Submitted to JAMES  Zorzetto, E. S. Malyshev, P. Ginoux, and E. Shevliakova (2024): A global land snow scheme (GLASS) v1.0 for the GFDL Earth System Model: formulation and evaluation at instrumented sites. Geoscientific Model Development, 17, No.19, 7219-7244, doi:10.5194/gmd-17-7219-2024. https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-17-7219-2024  Zorzetto, E, P. Ginoux, S. Malyshev, and E. Shevliakova (2025): Quantifying radiative effects of light-absorbing particle deposition on snow at the SnowMIP sites. The Cryosphere, 19, No.3, 1313–1334, doi:10.5194/tc-19-1313-2025.",
